{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And he loved also Rachel, more than Leah.</b> This implies that he loved Leah, only that he loved Rachel more than Leah — yet the verse says (Genesis 29:31), ״וַיַּרְא ה׳ כִּי שְׂנוּאָה לֵאָה״, 'and HaShem saw that Leah was hated.' And further, what is the meaning of 'also'? And it appears that since the superiority of wisdom comes from folly, like the superiority of light that comes from darkness — for no virtue is recognizable until one sees its absence and sees this opposite that, and then the superiority of wisdom and of light appears all the more — so too Jacob loved Rachel for her own sake, and beyond this love he added to her yet another love on account of Leah: for he saw this one opposite that one, and the superiority of Rachel was revealed all the more. This is what it says: 'And he loved also Rachel' — the word 'also' (גַּם) comes to include the additional love drawn toward Rachel from Leah, and the mem of מִלֵּאָה is not a mem of comparison/superiority. And some say that this additional love was drawn from Leah because she handed over her signs to Leah, and he considered this matter as righteousness on Rachel's part; therefore he added love to her."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:03.940102758Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}